Restore an enlarged bottom page with room for more rows too.

Ghostty a622196c restores a snapshot's bottom page with room for more rows
(PageList.fillPooled), but only when the page fits in a pool item. A page
enlarged past a standard page (more styles or graphemes) still restores at
the exact rows its record holds. It is full, so the first row written after a
restore starts a new page. Each snapshot, restore and write cycle then adds a
page that counts against the scrollback byte limit.

Give such a page as many rows as a standard page of its width holds, as a
terminal that never restored would have. The PAGE decoder already accepts a
destination with more rows.

Applied by `make libghostty` to the pinned Ghostty commit (GHOSTTY_COMMIT).

diff --git a/src/terminal/PageList.zig b/src/terminal/PageList.zig
index 122a3e6dc477..8f5f5565c7e4 100644
--- a/src/terminal/PageList.zig
+++ b/src/terminal/PageList.zig
@@ -5088,22 +5088,28 @@ pub fn increaseCapacity(
 
 /// Returns `cap` with more rows, enough to fill the memory a pooled page
 /// takes up anyway. If a page with `cap` is too large for the pool, this
-/// returns `cap` unchanged.
+/// returns `cap` with as many rows as a standard page of its width holds.
 ///
 /// Every page small enough for the pool gets a full `std_size` buffer,
 /// even when its capacity needs less. The leftover space can hold more
 /// rows at no extra cost. Use this when allocating a page that will keep
 /// receiving rows. A page allocated with only as many rows as it holds is
 /// already full, so the next `grow` has to allocate a whole new page.
 ///
 /// Only `rows` changes, and it never gets smaller.
 ///
 /// ```zig
 /// // Leave room for new output in a restored active page.
 /// const allocation = try list.allocatePage(PageList.fillPooled(capacity));
 /// ```
 pub fn fillPooled(cap: Capacity) Capacity {
-    if (Page.layout(cap).total_size > std_size) return cap;
+    if (Page.layout(cap).total_size > std_size) {
+        const std_rows = (std_capacity.adjust(.{ .cols = cap.cols }) catch return cap).rows;
+        var filled = cap;
+        filled.rows = @max(cap.rows, std_rows);
+        if (Page.layout(filled).total_size > size.max_page_size) return cap;
+        return filled;
+    }
     const filled = cap.fitRows(std_size) orelse return cap;
     assert(filled.rows >= cap.rows);
     return filled;
